    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mao-zedong.jpg" alt="Mao Zedong" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mao_Zedong_1950_Portrait_(3x4_cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chen Zhengqing (1917–1966), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The People’s Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1921. After its alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> collapsed in 1927, the party built rural base areas, proclaimed the Chinese Soviet Republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> in 1931 and, driven out by Nationalist encirclement campaigns, made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a> of 1934–35 to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, where Yan’an became its base, during which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> rose to the leadership. During the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1937 to 1945 the Communists greatly expanded their armies and the territory they held behind Japanese lines. When the civil war resumed in 1946, the People’s Liberation Army won the decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoshen-campaign">Liaoshen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaihai-campaign">Huaihai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pingjin-campaign">Pingjin campaigns</a> of 1948–49 and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> in April 1949. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-peoples-political-consultative-conference">Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference</a> adopted a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-program">Common Program</a> in September, and on 1 October 1949 Mao proclaimed the People’s Republic from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Gate of Heavenly Peace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, which again became the capital, while the Nationalist government withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. In 1950 the new state signed a treaty of friendship and alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chamdo">Battle of Chamdo</a> in October 1950 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> was incorporated under the Seventeen Point Agreement of 1951, completing control over almost all the mainland territory of the former Republic of China.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shenzhen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shenzhen.jpg" alt="Shenzhen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shenzhen">Shenzhen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Commercial_area_of_futian_to_east2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlie fong, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The new state transformed rural society through land reform between 1950 and 1953, which redistributed land to poor peasants and killed large numbers of landlords after mass accusation meetings, and then through collectivisation. The First Five-Year Plan of 1953–57, modelled on Soviet practice, built heavy industry with Soviet aid, while a household registration system, the <em>hukou</em>, tied people to their place of birth and divided town from countryside. In 1958 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao</a> launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a>, merging collectives into vast people’s communes and demanding impossible targets for grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>; the resulting famine of 1959–61 killed an estimated 15 to 45 million people and was probably the deadliest in recorded history. From 1978 the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> restored family farming under the household responsibility system, opened Special Economic Zones such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenzhen">Shenzhen</a> in 1980 and gradually admitted private enterprise and foreign investment. The one-child policy, introduced in 1980 and replaced by a two-child limit only in 2016, reshaped families and the age structure. Growth averaging close to ten per cent a year for three decades lifted hundreds of millions of people out of poverty, drew vast migration to the coastal cities and by 2010 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the world’s second-largest economy and its largest manufacturer and exporter of goods.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/korean-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/korean-war.jpg" alt="Korean War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chosin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo by Corporal Peter McDonald, USMC, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The People’s Liberation Army, the armed wing of the Communist Party rather than of the state, emerged from the civil war as one of the largest armies in the world. In October 1950 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-volunteer-army">Chinese People’s Volunteers</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peng-dehuai">Peng Dehuai</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>, drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> forces back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a> and fought them to a stalemate before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> of July 1953, at a cost of hundreds of thousands of Chinese dead. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in a brief border war in 1962, tested its first atomic bomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lop-nur">Lop Nur</a> on 16 October 1964 and its first hydrogen bomb in 1967, and launched its first satellite in 1970. Rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> led to armed clashes on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhenbao-island">Zhenbao Island</a> on the Ussuri River in 1969, and in 1979 the army invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in a short and costly campaign. Missile tests near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1995–96 provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-taiwan-strait-crisis">Third Taiwan Strait Crisis</a>. From the 1990s rapid economic growth funded sweeping modernisation: China commissioned its first aircraft carrier, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a></em>, in 2012, built artificial islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> from about 2014, restructured its forces in 2015–16 and by the 2020s had the largest navy in the world by number of ships. A clash with Indian troops in the Galwan Valley in June 2020 was the first fatal one on that border since 1975.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/liu-shaoqi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liu-shaoqi.jpg" alt="Liu Shaoqi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/liu-shaoqi">Liu Shaoqi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liu_Shaoqi_in_1959.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The People’s Republic is a party-state: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, through its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> and its Standing Committee, directs the government, the army and the mass organisations, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress">National People’s Congress</a> serves as the formal legislature. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, the party’s chairman until his death, dominated politics through a succession of mass campaigns. The Hundred Flowers invitation to criticism in 1956–57 was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-rightist-campaign">Anti-Rightist Campaign</a>, and after the failure of the Great Leap Forward Mao launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> in 1966, mobilising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-guards-china">Red Guards</a> against party leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shaoqi">Liu Shaoqi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> and against ‘old’ culture; estimates of those killed in persecution and factional violence range from several hundred thousand to two million or more. After Mao’s death in September 1976 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gang-of-four">Gang of Four</a>, led by his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-qing">Jiang Qing</a>, was arrested, and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-plenum-of-the-11th-central-committee">Third Plenum</a> of December 1978 Deng Xiaoping was the paramount leader. The constitution of 1982, collective leadership and retirement norms brought more predictable government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-zemin">Jiang Zemin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-jintao">Hu Jintao</a>, but the party kept its monopoly of power, crushing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1989-tiananmen-square-protests-and-massacre">Tiananmen Square protests</a> on 4 June 1989. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xi-jinping">Xi Jinping</a>, general secretary since 2012 and president since 2013, has recentralised authority, led a sweeping anti-corruption campaign, removed presidential term limits in 2018 and began a third term as party leader in 2022.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai.jpg" alt="Zhou Enlai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Premier_Chou_En-Lai_in_the_Great_Hall_of_the_People%27s_Banquet_Hall_-_DPLA_-_a40021a8100507bc7a244420481bf488.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">President (1969-1974 : Nixon). White House Photo Office. 1969-1974; General Services Administration. National Archive..., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The People’s Republic reshaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and the wider world. Its intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> established it as a military power, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Bandung Conference</a> of 1955 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> presented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as a partner of newly independent states in Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a>, open by the early 1960s, divided the communist world, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao</a>’s doctrine of rural revolution inspired movements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naxalites">Naxalites</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a>, while China backed communist forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a>’s visit in February 1972 began a realignment that led to full diplomatic relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1979. At home the state introduced simplified Chinese characters from 1956 and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanyu-pinyin">Hanyu Pinyin</a> romanisation in 1958, both now standard for learners of Chinese worldwide, and promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-chinese">Standard Mandarin</a> as the national language. Reform and opening after 1978 made China the centre of global manufacturing supply chains and transformed world commodity markets. In 2013 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xi-jinping">Xi Jinping</a> launched the Belt and Road Initiative, financing ports, railways and power plants across Asia, Africa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and China has become the largest trading partner of more than a hundred countries and the leading producer of solar panels, batteries and electric vehicles. Its rise is widely seen as the central change in the international order of the early twenty-first century.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Inventions and discoveries credited here</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/inventions?focus=artemisinin">Artemisinin<span>1972</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xi-jinping"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xi-jinping.jpg" alt="Xi Jinping" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xi-jinping">Xi Jinping</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prime_Minister_Keir_Starmer_visits_China_(55066713683)_(cropped%2Bangle).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Simon Dawson / No 10 Downing Street, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The People’s Republic still exists, and since 1949 it has been transformed from a poor, largely rural and isolated country into an urbanised industrial power without any change in its political system. It took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s seat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, including permanent membership of the Security Council, in October 1971. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> returned from British rule on 1 July 1997 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> from Portuguese rule on 20 December 1999, both as special administrative regions under the formula of ‘one country, two systems’. China joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-trade-organization">World Trade Organization</a> in December 2001, hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2008-summer-olympics">Summer Olympic Games</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 2008 and passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as the world’s second-largest economy in 2010, and around 2011 its urban population overtook the rural. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xi-jinping">Xi Jinping</a> control has tightened: from about 2017 large numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and other Muslims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> were held in detention camps, and a National Security Law imposed on Hong Kong in 2020 ended most organised opposition there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covid-19-pandemic">COVID-19</a> was first identified in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a> at the end of 2019, and strict ‘zero-COVID’ controls lasted until December 2022. The population began to fall in 2022, and by United Nations estimates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> passed China as the most populous country in 2023. The People’s Republic claims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, which it has never governed, and remains a one-party state.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The People’s Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> ended a century of division and war on the mainland, passed through the catastrophes of the Great Leap Forward and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, and after 1978 combined market reform with one-party rule to become the world’s second-largest economy and a great power.</em></p>

